<BaseLayout
  title="How to Read a Rapid Test Result — Control Line, Test Line | Progene Bio"
  description="How to interpret a lateral-flow rapid test result — control line (validity), test line (positive), and common reading errors."
  canonical="/knowledge/result-interpretation/"
>

  <h1>How to Read a Rapid Test Result</h1>

  <p><strong>Direct answer:</strong> A valid result shows a control line; a test line means positive; no control line means invalid — repeat with a new device.</p>

  <div class="card">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Lines</th><th>Result</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th>Control only</th><td>Negative</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Control + Test</th><td>Positive (reactive)</td></tr>
        <tr><th>No control line</th><td>Invalid — repeat with a new device</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2>Common errors</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Reading too late</strong> — a line appearing after the read window is invalid.</li>
    <li><strong>Ignoring the control line</strong> — no control = invalid, regardless of test line.</li>
    <li><strong>Treating faint line as negative</strong> — any visible test line within window = reactive.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>Always follow the product IFU; a reactive result is presumptive and may need confirmation.</p>

  <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
  <div class="card">
    <p><strong>What does a faint test line mean?</strong> Any visible test line, however faint, is typically read as positive — confirm per the product IFU.</p>
  </div>

